---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';

const contact = 'jorge.miguel.ferreira.silva@ua.pt';
const dpo = 'epd@ua.pt';
const link = 'text-brand-dark underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-brand-800';
const h2 = 'text-2xl font-medium text-text tracking-tight mt-12 mb-4';
const p = 'text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy notice · BioChef" description="Privacy notice for the BioChef Agent (test) LS Login client.">
  <PageHero
    kicker="Privacy"
    title="Privacy notice"
    subtitle="What the BioChef Agent (test) LS Login client receives when you log in, why, and for how long it is kept."
    crumbs={[{ label: 'Privacy' }]}
    meta={[
      { label: 'Applies to', value: 'BioChef Agent (test) LS Login client' },
      { label: 'Data controller', value: 'University of Aveiro (IEETA)' },
      { label: 'Last updated', value: '16 November 2026' }
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-main">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h2 class={h2.replace('mt-12 ', '')}>What this service is</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          This notice covers only the BioChef Agent (test) LS Login client. It does not cover this
          website or the BioChef app.
        </p>
        <p class={p}>
          "BioChef Agent (test)" is a research test of the BioChef project (ELIXIR HDTR 2027/28,
          Work Package 4), run at IEETA, University of Aveiro. It tests whether a software agent that
          runs inside a Trusted Research Environment can keep a researcher's consent to fetch data the
          researcher is already approved to use. It is registered only in the LS Login test
          environment, so only users who have joined that test environment can log in. In practice
          these are members of the BioChef team, with their own accounts.
        </p>

        <h2 class={h2}>Who is responsible</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          The data controller is the University of Aveiro, through its research unit IEETA. Contact: <a class={link} href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>.
        </p>
        <p class={p}>
          The University of Aveiro's Data Protection Officer can be reached at <a class={link} href={`mailto:${dpo}`}>{dpo}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class={h2}>What data we receive</h2>
        <p class={p}>When you log in through LS Login, this service receives only:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <li>your LS Login user identifier (the "sub" value);</li>
          <li>
            your GA4GH Passport, that is, the visas LS Login holds for you, such as affiliations and
            dataset access permissions;
          </li>
          <li>
            an access token and a refresh token that let the agent ask LS Login for your visas again
            later.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class={p}>We do not request your name or email address.</p>

        <h2 class={h2}>Why we use it</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          Only to test the login and consent mechanism: whether LS Login issues a refresh token to
          this client, how long it lasts, and whether it changes when used. We do not use the data for
          any other purpose, and we do not fetch any research data with it during this test.
        </p>

        <h2 class={h2}>Legal basis</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          The consent of the team members who take part in the test (Article 6(1)(a) of the General
          Data Protection Regulation).
        </p>

        <h2 class={h2}>Who receives it</h2>
        <p class={p}>The data is not shared with anyone.</p>

        <h2 class={h2}>Where it is kept and for how long</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          To measure how long a refresh token lasts, the tokens must be kept for several days. They are
          stored encrypted on the tester's own computer until the test ends, are never written to
          logs, and are deleted when the service is removed from LS Login.
        </p>
        <p class={p}>
          To investigate security incidents, as LS Login asks of every connected service, we keep a
          minimal access log (time of login and your LS Login identifier, no tokens) for 180 days,
          and then delete it.
        </p>
        <p class={p}>
          The test lasts one month from the approval of the registration and may be extended if
          needed. When it ends, the service is removed from LS Login.
        </p>
        <p class={p}>If someone outside the team logs in, their data is deleted immediately and is not used.</p>

        <h2 class={h2}>Your rights</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          You have the right to access your data, have it rectified or erased, restrict its
          processing, receive it in a portable format, and withdraw your consent at any time. To do
          any of these, write to <a class={link} href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>.
          You can also revoke this service's access in your LS Login account at any time. You may
          complain to the Portuguese data protection authority (CNPD).
        </p>

        <h2 class={h2}>LS Login</h2>
        <p class={p}>
          The login itself is run by LS Login. Its own privacy notice covers the data it processes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
